# Generated documents are never hand-edited and never tracked — any report can
# be deleted and regenerated identically from the store.
reports/

# Local database files (Postgres is the runtime store; SQLite is for tests).
*.db
*.sqlite3

# Local virtualenv
.venv/

# Compiled bytecode. Interpreter output, rebuilt on the next import, and a
# `git add -A` after any test run otherwise sweeps hundreds of files into a
# commit. This heading deliberately says nothing about secrets: the ignore file
# is parsed section by section by tests/test_docker_build_context.py and
# tests/test_docs_no_secrets_in_repo.py, which take the sections whose own
# comment names a secret as the list of things that must never be built or
# documented — a bytecode rule filed under such a heading would silently widen
# what those two guards enforce.
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]

# Rendered page snapshots (bin/driftless-snapshot-pages.py). Regenerable output,
# like reports/ above: the whole bundle re-renders byte-identically from the demo
# store at a pinned as-of, so committing 38 pages would only rot on the next
# template change. This heading deliberately names nothing an intruder would
# want, for the reason the bytecode heading above states.
docs/page-snapshots/

# Coverage artifacts
.coverage
htmlcov/

# setuptools build output. A non-editable `pip install .` (which is what CI
# runs) leaves a build/lib copy of the package in the checkout; tracking it
# would also give mypy two modules with the same name.
build/
dist/
*.egg-info/

# The deployment's age PRIVATE key. Neither the encrypted secrets overlay nor
# the private key that decrypts it is committed: both are written on the
# deployment host, the key outside the checkout entirely (OPERATIONS.md
# generates it under ~/.config/driftless, and tests/test_docs_no_secrets_in_repo.py
# fails any document that says otherwise). These patterns are the backstop for a
# key that lands here anyway. Backups are written locally, too.
deploy/age-key.txt
deploy/*.key
backups/
